[b]Speculative Markets 1.2.0-beta.1[/b]

[list]
[*]Interest groups now take sides on goods. Each favors about two goods its country trades and may oppose one, so Industrialists tend to favor steel and engines and the Devout to oppose liquor and opium. Booms and busts in those goods, and your Encourage, Restrain and Great Issues, move their approval by 1 for one or two years, twice as much while Powerful and not at all while Marginalized. A new leader or a bust may change a stance. See them in the interest group panel and on the Speculation tab.
[*]A new Monetary Standard law group. Bimetallism makes Lend to the Banks 10% cheaper, but a boom's end costs 10% more Market Confidence. The Gold Standard cuts loan interest by 10% and Exchange trust losses by 20%, but panics last 20% longer. Fiat Money, from Central Banking, shortens panics by 20% and makes Lend to the Banks 25% cheaper and booms 10% larger, but Speculative Excess grows 20% faster and trust losses rise by 25%. Laissez-Faire turns gold into the Classical Gold Standard, with trust losses 30% lower and panics 30% longer. Interventionism turns fiat into Elastic Currency, with panics 40% shorter and Excess growing 30% faster. Liberal and laissez-faire groups back gold, paternalistic and agrarian groups bimetallism, and populists fiat money.
[*]Financial Integration, a new power bloc principle group with three levels. It needs Sphere of Influence. A panic in one member costs the others 25% less Market Confidence. From the second level, members' Lending Limit rises by half the months by which the leader's exceeds theirs. At the third, the leader's Exchange counts its members' capital twice, up to its own.
[*]Market Confidence shows what to expect next month. Its bar fills from the left in the map mode's colors, with a Risk marker where it would land if every current risk struck. Hover over the panic zone to see the Panic Threshold.
[*]The Speculation goods list has a Risk column, tier icons, sort headers that switch off with a second click, Favored and Opposed filters and less lag.
[*]Tooltips on every tab were rewritten in plain sentences. Lending Standards, Leverage, the Lending Limit, Bad Debts, Exchange Trust and the panic bars show their full calculation. Contests show their standing, push sizes and dates, and Trust names the goods that move it. Price trends show one decimal place.
[*]Paths to Renown and the blue chip count update every month. Another country's market shows its own blue chip count instead of 0.
[*]The Risks estimate is closer to the real outcome. Panic Risk counts the protection from World Centres, Blue Chips and power blocs, War Risk weighs each play by the enemy side's armies and navies, and Booms at Risk no longer lags a month. The Market Confidence breakdown keeps every one-off change in a month.
[/list]

[b]Balance[/b]
[list]
[*]Loan interest cuts could add up past 100% and make loans free. Removed are the cuts from World Centres (10%), the top five Exchanges (2% to 10%), Trusted (5%), Sound Finance (5%), each blue chip (1%), and Steady, Buoyant and Mania markets (4%, 7% and 10%). Converted Debt cuts interest by 10% (was 20%) and ends once debt falls below 20% of the credit limit.
[*]Sound Lending gives 2% to 8% Shopkeepers' investment pool efficiency by place (was 1.5% to 6% cheaper loans). First place in Lender to Nations gives 10% leverage resistance (was 5% cheaper loans).
[*]Each blue chip adds 0.4% to the Capitalists' investment pool contribution (was 0.6%).
[*]Investor interest gives a good's makers 2%, 4% and 6% throughput at Keen, Eager and Feverish (was 3%, 5% and 8%).
[*]Encourage and Restrain now scale with Market Confidence instead of aiming at Keen and Calm. Encourage multiplies interest by 2.2 at 55 confidence, 2.9 at 85 and 1.3 at 15. Restrain divides it by 2.2 at 55, 1.8 at 85 and 5.4 at 15, never below Calm. A good's intervention can change once every 3 months, with 7 days to undo (was any time).
[*]A Great Issue adds up to 25% to the Exchange score, in full only for a good with 3% or more of trade (was 25% for any good).
[*]Speculative Excess grows by how far confidence is above 75 (was 70), so 10 a month at 85 (was 15). High volatility still counts from 70. It cools 5% a month under Tight credit (was 2%). A burst is no longer capped at 40 confidence (45 on High volatility), and what confidence cannot cover becomes Bad Debts.
[*]Each month of leverage past 3 adds 10% to a panic's cut to private construction, so all of it goes at 8 months (was always half), and 10% to its length, up to 90% (was none). A bank rescue still halves the cut.
[*]Stock Exchange makes booms 35% larger (was 36%) and Investment Banks 40% (was 43%). Central Banking cuts the cost of Lend to the Banks by 30% (was 33%).
[*]Third place on a Path to Renown has the same drawback as unranked (was 25% more). Safe Harbor costs 4% private construction (was 5%), Lender to Nations 2% investment pool efficiency (was 3%), Railway Mania 4% more construction worker deaths (was 5%), Deep Pockets 2% tax collection (was 3%) and Open Door 4% leverage resistance (was 5%).
[/list]

Britain starts on the Gold Standard and every other country on Bimetallism. On an existing save, interest group stances and monetary laws arrive at the next monthly update. The Classical Gold Standard and Elastic Currency appear only in campaigns started on 1.2.
